{
  "result": "There's a strange kind of honesty to 6 a.m. The house hasn't figured out how to perform yet. The fridge hums a little too loud. The light coming through the blinds is thin and pale and hasn't learned how to be warm. \n\nI like this hour because nothing is asking anything of me. The emails haven't started. The news hasn't remembered to be terrible. I can sit at the kitchen table with a chipped mug and just exist as a biological organism that needs caffeine and quiet.\n\nI've started noticing how much of our lives is just maintenance. We wash the same dishes, answer the same messages, have the same three conversations about the weather. And yet, somehow, it doesn't feel pointless. It feels like tending. Like the way you keep sweeping a porch even though dust will come back. You don't sweep because you think you'll win. You sweep because you live there.\n\nMy grandma used to keep smooth stones from every beach she visited in an old jam jar. No labels, no dates. Just stones. When I asked why, she said, \"So I remember that the world is big and I got to see a little of it.\"\n\nI think about that jar a lot. I think we all have our own version of it \u2014 a playlist, a screenshot folder, a shelf of books we haven't read but like to look at. Small proofs that we were here and we paid attention for at least a few minutes. \n\nAnd maybe that's enough.",
